  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 PM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery reveals partial clearing across the\nregion today, which has allowed surface temperatures to recover\nslightly into the 70s across lower elevations. This solar \nheating, combined with an entrenched plume of deep monsoonal \nmoisture, has initiated another round of showers and isolated \nthunderstorms. While convective coverage will remain highest \nacross the Central Mountains and Eastern Highlands, scattered \nshowers and storms will also track across the Snake Plain into \nthe evening hours. High precipitable water values will make the \nstrongest cells capable of localized flash flooding, with recent\nburn scars facing the highest vulnerability. A Flash Flood \nWatch remains in effect for both the Wapiti and Big Rock burn \nscars through this evening with the former location continuing \ninto the evening tomorrow. Convective activity is forecast to \ndiminish overnight before ramping back up toward daybreak \nSaturday.\n\nAtmospheric conditions will remain active on Saturday as the \nremnant shortwave of a West Coast trough lifts northeastward \nacross Idaho. Model guidance continues to highlight the Central \nMountains for the most concentrated shower and thunderstorm \nactivity. Convective coverage elsewhere will be more scattered \nas the upper-level flow transitions from southwesterly to \nwesterly, keeping the primary forcing mechanism aligned closer \nto the northern terrain. Because deep moisture remains locked \nin place over the Central Mountains, the threat of heavy \nrainfall over the Wapiti burn scar remains high enough to \nmaintain the Flash Flood Watch on Saturday. Confidence in \norganized thunderstorm coverage directly impacting the Big Rock \nscar is lower at this time.\n\nA transitional pattern begins Sunday as the primary monsoonal\nmoisture plume shifts eastward, offering a mostly dry finish to\nthe weekend for much of Eastern Idaho outside of isolated to\nscattered central mountain showers and storms. Early next week,\na weak shortwave passing to our north on Monday and Tuesday \nwill maintain elevated precipitation chances from the Central \nMountains through the Island Park area, while locations further \nsouth see only isolated coverage. Behind this wave, drier air \nwill build into the Intermountain West for the mid-to-late week \ntimeframe, suppressing widespread shower activity. Daytime \ntemperatures \u2014 currently running well below seasonal averages \ndue to cloud cover and rain \u2014 will steadily moderate through \nearly next week, returning close to normal levels by late next \nweek as the drier weather takes hold.",
